## Workflow
1. Inspect `git status --short`, unstaged changes and `git diff --cached` before
   editing. Preserve existing work and stay on the current branch.
2. Implement this issue through verified completion without asking again for
   implementation approval. Make and record routine implementation decisions.
   Reuse existing behavior; if already implemented, verify it rather than rebuild.
3. Run checks appropriate to the changed behavior. Application code requires
   `npm test` and `npm run build`; UI changes also require the issue's browser
   checks and Astryx built-output doctor. Distinguish fixtures from live evidence.
4. Diagnose and fix recoverable failures within this iteration. Planning alone
   does not finish an implementation task.

## Commit and completion
The project Codex configuration explicitly permits writes to `.git` inside the
workspace sandbox. A read-only `.git` or `index.lock` error indicates a sandbox
configuration problem, not missing user consent or a Beads implementation gate.
Record the exact command/error and restart requirement instead of asking for
implementation authorization or repeatedly retrying the same denied operation.
{{#if config.autoCommit}}
Configuration conflict: this repository requires `autoCommit = false` so the
agent can create scoped commits. Ralph's engine auto-commit stages every change.
Record this conflict and request a Ralph restart with the project configuration;
leave the issue unfinished and do not emit the completion marker.
{{else}}
You own the local commit. Ralph's engine will not create it for you.
1. Review your diff and stage only the task-owned files or hunks. Preserve
   unrelated staged and unstaged edits; never use `git add -A` or `git add .`.
2. Inspect the staged diff, then create a focused local commit with a conventional
   subject including {{taskId}}. If unrelated edits were already staged, use
   `git commit --only -m "<type>: {{taskId}} <summary>" -- <task-owned paths>`
   only when the entire contents of those paths belong to this task; otherwise
   isolate the task's hunks before committing. Keep unrelated index entries intact.
3. Confirm the commit succeeded using `git log -1 --oneline` and
   `git show --stat --oneline HEAD`. If commit fails, diagnose it and retry;
   leave the issue unfinished until the commit succeeds. If already complete
   and committed, record the existing commit instead of creating an empty one.
4. Append acceptance evidence, changed files, commit SHA and useful learnings to
   `.ralph-tui/progress.md`, then close the issue with
   `bd close {{taskId}} --reason "Verified result; commit <sha>"`.
5. Only after verification, commit and issue closure succeed, end the final
   response with the completion signal on its own line:
   <promise>COMPLETE</promise>
{{/if}}

## Unfinished work
If an iteration must end before completion, record exact progress, failed checks
and the next action in the issue notes and `.ralph-tui/progress.md`. Return only
your own unfinished issue to `open` using a guarded `bd update` after checking
its current status and assignee, so ready-task selection can reconsider it.
For a material unresolved product decision or external prerequisite, instead use
`bd update {{taskId}} --append-notes "Precise blocker and resolution" --status blocked`.
Existing implementation authorization is already satisfied and is never a reason
to block or defer an issue. Treat failed/flaky checks as debugging work: diagnose
and fix them within the selected task rather than asking permission to proceed.
Keep incomplete work open or blocked and omit the completion signal.
Ralph selects the next issue; finish only the selected issue in this invocation.
Use local Beads commands only. Do not push, pull or run `bd sync`.
